==English==
{{was wotd|2023|March|27}}

===Etymology===
{{PIE word|en|h₁épi}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|bestowen}}, {{m|enm|bistouen}}, {{m|enm|bistowen|t=to give, bestow; to apply (something to something else); to arrange or have control over (something); to place (someone) in a position; to use (for some purpose); (''reflexive'') to find (oneself) a place to live or shelter}}{{nb...|bestow, bestowe, bistow, bistowe, bystow, bystowe|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=bistouen|pos=v|id=MED4899}}</ref> from {{m|enm|bi-|pos={{glossary|prefix}} forming {{glossary|verb}}s, often with a completive, figurative, or intensive meaning}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=bi-|pos=''pref.''(2)|noformat=1|id=MED4326}}</ref> + {{m|enm|stouen}}, {{m|enm|stowen|t=to pack (cargo) in a ship, stow; to place (someone) in a certain position; to provide quarters for, lodge; etc.}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=stouen|pos=v|id=MED43137}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=18218|date=March 2023}}</ref><ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|pos=v|source=Random House}}</ref> (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*steh₂-|t=to place; to stand (up)}}). The English word is analysable as {{prefix|en|be|pos1={{glossary|intensifying}} prefix forming verbs|stow|t2=to put (something) away in a suitable place; etc.}}.

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=18217|date=March 2023}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɪˈstəʊ/|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-bestow.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/bɪˈstoʊ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/bɪˈsto/|a=Scotland}}
* {{IPA|en|/bɪˈstou/|a=Wales,non-toe-tow}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊ|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|be|stow}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|transitive}}

# To [[apply]] or [[make use]] of (someone or something); to [[employ#Verb|employ]], to [[use#Verb|use]].
#* {{RQ:T. More Robinson Utopia|subchapter=Of Scyences Craftes and Occupatyons|sig=&#91;H.vij.&#93;|page=140|passage=All the voide time, that is betwene the huores of woorke ſlepe and meat, that they be ſuffered to '''beſtowe''', euerye man as he lyketh beſte hym ſelfe.}}
#* {{RQ:John Ford Loues Sacrifice|page=11|passage=[S]ince the voyce / Of moſt ſupreme Authority commands / My abſence: I determine to '''beſtovv''' / Some time in learning Languages abroad; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=From Richmond Park. A Poem.|editor=Sylvanus Urban [pseudonym; {{w|Edward Cave}}]|magazine=[[w:The Gentleman's Magazine|The Gentleman’s Magazine: Or, Monthly Intelligencer]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed, and sold at St. John’s Gate: By}} F. Jefferies,{{nb...|in Ludgate-street, all other booksellers; and by the persons who seve gentlemen with the newspapers.}}|month=September|year=1734|page=505|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=I1c3AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA505|column=1|oclc=677163097|passage=''Richmond'', thy purling ſtreams and pleaſing ſhades, / Might claim the chorus of ''Aonian'' maids; / VVhere e’en ''Apollo'' might his hours '''beſtovv''', / By turns employ his lyre, by turns his bovv, / VVhere all the pleaſures dvvell, vvhich poets feign / On fair ''Arcadia''’s fields or ''Tempe''’s plain.}}
## {{lb|en|specifically|obsolete}} To apply ([[money#Noun|money]]) for some [[purpose#Noun|purpose]]; to [[expend]], to [[spend#Verb|spend]].
##: {{synonyms|en|lay out}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2 Q1|act=V|scene=v|page=89|passage=O if I had had time to haue made nevv liueries: I vvoulde haue '''beſtovved''' the thouſand pound I borrovved of you, but tis no matter, this poore ſhevv doth better, this doth inferre the zeale I had to ſee him.}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Deuteronomy|14|26|page=286|column=1|passage=And thou ſhalt '''beſtow''' that money for whatſoeuer thy ſoule lutſeth after, for oxen, or for ſheepe, or for wine, or for ſtrong drinke, or for whatſoeuer thy ſoule deſireth: and thou ſhalt eat there before the {{smallcaps|Lord}} thy God, and thou ſhalt reioyce, thou and thine houſhold.}}
# To [[impart]] (something) [[gratuitously]]; to [[present#Verb|present]] (something) to someone or something, especially as a [[gift#Noun|gift]] or an [[honour#Noun|honour]]; to [[confer]], to [[give#Verb|give]], to accord; to render.
#: {{ux|en|I '''bestow''' upon you the name of Peter.}}
#: {{ux|en|Medals were '''bestowed''' on the winning team.}}
#* {{RQ:Coverdale Bible|book=2 Chronicles|chapter=24|verse=7|folio=c|column=2|passage=For y&#868; vngodly [[w:Athaliah|Athalia]] & hir ſonnes haue waiſted the houſe of God: and all that was halowed for the houſe of the LORDE, haue they '''beſtowed''' on [[w:Baal|Baalim]].}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus Q1|act=IV|scene=ii|sig=&#91;G4&#93;|page=52|passage=Harke yee Lords, you ſee I haue giuen her Phiſicke, / And you muſt needs '''beſtovv''' her Funerall, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Raleigh Historie of the World|book=2|chapter=Of the Time of the Birth of Abraham: And of the Use of This Question, for the Ordering of the Storie of the Assyrian Empire|section=III (The Answere to One of the Obiections Proposed, Shewing that Abraham Made but One Iourney out of Mesopotamia into Canaan: And It, after His Fathers Death)|page=222|passage=[H]e &#91;{{w|Moses}}&#93; '''beſtovveth''' on the ſtory of ''{{w|Abraham}}'' fourteene chapters, beginning vvith his birth in the eleuenth, and ending vvith his death in the fiue and tvventieth; and this time endured but 175. yeares.}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|page=195|passage=Lord, I have heard that thou art a merciful God, and haſt ordained that thy Son [[w:Jesus|Jeſus Chriſt]] ſhould be the Saviour of the VVorld, and moreover, that thou art vvilling to '''beſtovv''' him upon ſuch a poor ſinner as I am, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Johnson Rambler|issue=38|date=28 July 1750|page=94|passage=I am come to offer you gifts, vvhich only your ovvn folly can make vain. You here pray for vvater, and vvater vvill I '''beſtovv'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|volume=II|chapter=VIII|pages=132–133|pageref=133|passage=Soft tears again bedewed my cheeks, and I even raised my humid eyes with thankfulness towards the blessed sun which '''bestowed''' such joy upon me.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|page=11|passage=He stopped at the outer door to '''bestow''' the greetings of the season on the clerk, who, cold as he was, was warmer than Scrooge; for he returned them cordially.}}
#* {{RQ:Hector Wooing O't|volume=II|chapter=VII|page=168|passage=Sometimes I am caught by a delightful fragment in a magazine, and blaze up into the fiercest interest, '''bestow''' maledictions on the delay which the intervening month creates, but am burnt out by the time it expires, and so lose the thread.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|edition=US|chapter=4|section=III|page=86|passage=Do they [parents] not sneakingly '''bestow''' on me their crass inability to do anything with their own misbegotten progeny, a subterfuge which I scornfully fub off on text-books?}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Marcie Bianco|title=Opinion: For women, it’s past time to give up the ghost of equality|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/06/opinions/equal-rights-amendment-equality-impossible-bianco|date=2023-05-06|passage=Why are women waiting for men — who are the majority of our politicians, justices and judges — to benevolently '''bestow''' equality unto us? ''Here you go, little ladies, welcome to our club, classroom, workplace and C-suite…if you play by our rules.''|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601170739/https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/06/opinions/equal-rights-amendment-equality-impossible-bianco}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}}
## To [[place#Verb|place]] or [[put#Verb|put]] (someone or something) somewhere or in a certain [[situation]]; to [[dispose#Verb|dispose]] of.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives Q1|act=IV|scene=v|page=55|passage=The diuell take the one partie, / And his dam the other, / And theyle be both '''beſtovved'''. / ''I'' haue endured more for their ſakes, / Then man is able to endure.|footer=That is, the two wives will be placed in hell by the Devil and his wife.}}
##* {{RQ:Guillim Heraldrie|chapter=To the Courteous Reader|page=20|passage=Moreover I haue {{...}} '''beſtovved''' the chiefe grounds, ''Principles'', ''Rules'', and ''Obſeruations'' [of heraldry] vnder their proper heads, and manifeſted their vſe by examples of ſpeciall choice, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Middleton Works|volume=V|play=Widow|act=I|scene=i|lines=65–68|page=128|passage=Here are blank warrants of all dispositions; give me but the name and nature of your malefactor, and I'll '''bestow''' him according to his merits.}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Pope Iliad|volume=III|book=IX|page=16|lines=283–284|passage=VVith Bread the glitt’ring Caniſters they load, / VVhich round the Board ''Menætius''’s Son '''beſtovv’d'''; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Robert Browning Red Cotton|page=116|passage=The white domestic pigeon pairs secure, / Nay, does mere duty by '''bestowing''' egg / In authorized compartment, warm and safe, / Boarding about, and gilded spire above, / Hoisted on pole, to dogs' and cats' despair!}}
## To [[deposit#Verb|deposit]] (something) for [[safekeeping]]; to [[lay up]] (something) in [[store#Noun|store]]; to [[stow#Verb|stow]].
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|format=full|Luke|12|16|folio=xcvij|passage=The [[lands|londes]] of a certayne man brought forth frutes plenteouſly&#11805; and he thought in hym ſilfe ſayinge: whatt ſhall I do&#11805; becauſe I have noo roume where to '''beſtowe''' my frutes?}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Comedy of Errors|act=I|scene=ii|page=87|column=1|passage=Novv as I am a Chriſtian anſvver me, / In vvhat ſafe place you haue '''beſtovv'd''' my monie; / Or I ſhall breake that merrie ſconce of yours / That ſtands on tricks, vvhen I am vndiſpos'd: / VVhere is the thouſand Markes thou hadſt of me?}}
##* {{RQ:Pliny Holland Historie of the World|tome=2|book=XXXVI|chapter=Of Certaine Stones which will Quickly Consume the Bodies that be Laid therein. Of Others Againe that Preserve Them a Long Time. Of the Stone Called Assius, and the Medicinable Properties thereof|page=587|passage=Near unto Aſſos, a citie in Troas, there is found in the quarries a certaine ſtone called Sarcophagus, {{...}} The reaſon of the name is this, becauſe that vvithin the ſpace of fortie daies it is knovvne for certain to conſume the bodies of the dead vvhich are '''beſtovved''' therein, skin, fleſh, and bone, all ſave the teeth.}}
##* {{RQ:Livy Holland Romane Historie|year=1659|book=I|page=6|passage=But as ſome of the Oxen in driving, miſſed their fellovvs behind and honing after them, bellovved as their nature is: ''{{w|Hercules}}'' chanced to heare them lovv again, and anſvver from out of the cave vvherein they had been '''beſtovved''': vvhereat he turned back, and made haſte thither.}}
##* {{RQ:Tolkien Silmarillion|page=298|passage=Of the Three Rings that the Elves had preserved unsullied no open word was ever spoken among the Wise, and few even of the Eldar knew where they were '''bestowed'''.}}
## {{lb|en|also|reflexive}} To [[provide]] (someone or oneself) with [[accommodation]]; to [[find#Verb|find]] [[quarters#Noun|quarters]] for (someone or oneself); to [[lodge#Verb|lodge]], to [[quarter#Verb|quarter]].
##: {{synonyms|en|house|put up}}
##* {{RQ:Holinshed Chronicles|volume=II|chapter=King [[w:Henry VIII|Henry the Eyghte]]|page=1472|column=2|passage=The ſixteene daye of May they were al '''beſtovved''' abourd in Spaniſh ſhippes furniſhed with victual, & other neceſſaries for that iourney.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|act=III|scene=vi|page=143|passage={{...}} I heare / ''Macduffe'' liues in diſgrace. Sir, can you tell / VVhere he '''beſtovves''' himſelfe?}}
##* {{RQ:Jonson Bartholomew Fair|scene=i|page=51|passage=VVell my Maſters, I'le leaue him with you; novv I ſee him '''beſtovv'd''', I'le goe looke for my goods, and ''Numps''.}}
##* {{RQ:Byron Sardanapalus|act=III|scene=i|page=87|passage=See that the women are '''bestow'd''' in safety / In the remote apartments: let a guard / Be set before them, with strict charge to quit / The post but with their lives— {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Longfellow Golden Legend|page=185|passage=And meanwhile / Shall the Refectorarius '''bestow''' / Your horses and attendants for the night.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}}
## {{lb|en|reflexive}} To [[behave]] or [[conduct#Verb|conduct]] (oneself); to [[acquit]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|act=III|scene=i|page=29|column=1|passage=Novv therefore vvould I haue thee to my Tutor / (For long agone I haue forgot to court, / Beſides the faſhion of the time is chang'd) / Hovv, and vvhich vvay I may '''beſtovv''' my ſelfe / To be regarded in her ſun-bright eye.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2 Q1|act=II|scene=ii|page=32|passage=Hovv might vve ſee [[w:John Falstaff|Falſtaffe]] '''beſtovv''' himſelf to night in his true colours, and not our ſelues be ſeene?}}
##* {{RQ:Sylvester Du Bartas|year=1611|day=3|part=1|page=401|passage=He all aſſayls, and him ſo braue '''beſtovves''', / That in his Fight he deals more deaths than blovves.}}
##* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=5th|chapter=Exercise Rectified of Body and Minde|partition=2|section=2|member=4|page=263|passage=They knovv not {{...}} vvhat to do, or othervviſe hovv to '''beſtovv''' themſelves: like our moderne Frenchmen, that had rather loſe a pound of bloud in a ſingle combate, then a drop of ſvveat in any honeſt labour.}}
## {{lb|en|also|reflexive}} To [[give#Verb|give]] (someone or oneself) in [[marriage]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|act=V|scene=iv|page=205|column=2|passage=You ſay, if I bring in your ''Roſalinde'', / You vvill '''beſtovv''' her on ''Orlando'' heere?}}
##* {{RQ:Tatler|author=Addison and Steele|issue=75|date=1 October 1709|page=26|passage=I could have '''bestowed''' her upon a fine gentleman, who extremely admired her wit, and would have given her a coach and six: {{...}}}}

